From b9a391705079e7aabd2ca519718d90fd4e58ac91 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: danielhanchen <danielhanchen@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 01:19:54 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] rpc: direct server-to-server copies of split inputs

With pipeline parallelism over several rpc-servers every activation that
crosses from server A to server B was relayed by the client: a GET_TENSOR
from A followed by a SET_TENSOR to B, both on the client's link, and the
group thread blocked on the GET before it could queue anything for B.

Implement cpy_tensor_async for RPC backends on different endpoints. The
client queues PEER_PUSH(src, tag) on A's FIFO and PEER_AWAIT(tag, dst) on
B's FIFO under one lock, so the push follows the graph producing src, the
await precedes the graph consuming dst, and every server sees transfers in
one global order (no deadlock: an await only waits for a push with nothing
blocking ahead of it). No client round trip is added and the group thread
no longer waits on the inter-stage GET.

Server side:
- PEER_CONNECT opens (or reuses) a link from A to B. It uses the same
  mutual TLS 1.3 as client links: A presents its own server certificate and
  pins B's fingerprint from the tls://<fp>@host:port endpoint the client
  already uses; B admits A only if A is in its --tls-allow file. Plaintext
  links only between plaintext servers on 127.0.0.0/8.
- PEER_PUSH reads the tensor in command order and hands the bytes to the
  link's sender thread, so A can compute the next graph meanwhile.
- The accept loop runs on its own thread and routes a connection by its
  first command: HELLO to the one-client-at-a-time serve loop, PEER_HELLO
  to a receiver thread that files PEER_DATA by (source, tag). PEER_AWAIT
  waits for the entry and writes it like SET_TENSOR, so B receives while it
  computes.
- An allowlist line "<fp> peer" admits a certificate for peer transfers
  only; it cannot send HELLO and drive the server as a client.

The feature is advertised in the HELLO reply's former padding byte, which
older servers send as zero; the protocol version is unchanged, so old
clients and old servers keep working and pairs with an old server, a
refused link or GGML_RPC_P2P=0 (client or server) use the relay path.

Failures do not hang: an await fails when its source's link closes, the
client hangs up or after GGML_RPC_P2P_TIMEOUT seconds (default 120); a
push fails when the link failed; either drops the client connection. Links
close when the client session that opened them ends, so a failure travels
down the pipeline to whichever server the client waits on. The P2P server
ignores SIGPIPE so a dead peer fails a write instead of killing it.

Traces: rpc.peer PEER_SEND / PEER_RECV events with bytes and tag.
