From 84303f95fba3ca6c3fe31f23f954c55b1eb4ab7d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Han <danielhanchen@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 07:54:33 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] rpc: bound the P2P accept and peer receive paths

With P2P on, every accepted connection got its own detached thread before
TLS authentication, the read timeout was cleared after the first command
byte (so a PEER_HELLO that never sends its body held a thread forever),
and a PEER_DATA receiver allocated up to 4 GiB before the shared inbox
budget was checked, so concurrent incomplete messages bypassed it.

- at most GGML_RPC_P2P_MAX_CONN (default 64) connections are being
  authenticated or held as peer links at once; beyond it new connections
  are closed (logged once per saturation), and a failed thread creation
  closes that connection instead of terminating the server
- the TLS handshake, the first command and the whole PEER_HELLO share one
  absolute 10 s deadline, enforced by a watchdog thread that shuts down
  the socket (a per-recv timeout alone lets a byte-at-a-time sender stay)
- a PEER_DATA reserves its inbox budget before its buffer is allocated
  and releases it on failure; once started it must arrive whole within
  GGML_RPC_P2P_TIMEOUT seconds

Client connections and GGML_RPC_P2P=0 are unchanged.
